===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who is neither a [[he]] nor a [[she]]; a [[creature]]; a [[dehumanize]]d being.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=w:Herman Cyril McNeile|title=w:Bulldog Drummond| chapter=1| passage=His master glanced up quickly, and removed the letter from his hands. "I'm surprised at you, James," he remarked severely. "A secretary should control itself. Don't forget that the perfect secretary is an '''it''': an automatic machine—a thing incapable of feeling.…"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Neil Weiner; Sharon E. Robinson Kurpius| title=Shattered innocence| page=8| passage=Too often, children become an "'''it'''" in their homes and their humanness is devalued.}}
# {{senseid|en|tag}} The [[person]] who [[chase]]s and tries to [[catch]] the other [[player]]s in the [[playground]] game of [[tag]].
#: {{ux|en|In the next game, Adam and Tom will be '''it'''…}}
#: {{ux|en|Tag, you're '''it'''!}}
#* {{quote-book |en |author={{w|Clifton Johnson (author)|Clifton Johnson}} |page=66 |chapter=[[:s:en:What They Say in New England/Odds|Odds]] |title=[[:s:en:What They Say in New England|What They Say in New&nbsp;England]] |text=When you play hi-spy, and are “'''it''',” and want to know where the others have hid, take a stick and put it up on end and let it fall. If it falls three times in the same direction, that shows you the way to go to find the hiders. |year=1896 |publisher=Lee and Shepard Publishers |location=Boston, Massachusetts, United States |genre=non-fiction}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Katherine T. Thomas; Amelia M. Lee; Jerry R. Thomas|title=Physical education for children|page=464| passage=When there are only two children left who haven't been tagged, I will stop the game, and we will start over with those children starting as the '''Its'''.}}
#* {{quote-video game|1=en|year=2001|developer={{w|Intelligent Systems}}|publisher={{w|Nintendo}}|title={{w|Paper Mario (video game)|Paper Mario}}|system={{w|Nintendo 64}}|text=We were playing hide-and-seek. I had to be "'''it'''", but I couldn't find anyone at all.}}
# {{lb|en|British}} A game of [[tag]].
#: {{ux|en|Let's play '''it''' at breaktime.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[desirable]] characteristic, as being [[fashionable]].
#: {{ux|en|Man, he's really got '''it'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|She's the '''it''' girl, at least for this Fall.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|chiefly in the negative}} Something [[desirable]] or [[suitable]].
#: {{ux|en|Bro, that shirt is not '''it'''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Sexual intercourse.
#: {{ux|en|OMG, they were doing '''it''' in the storage room.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Sex appeal.
#: {{ux|en|She really has '''it''' going on.}}
# {{alt case|en|It||force in the [[vitalist]] approach of {{w|Georg Groddeck}}}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=Frederic D. Homer|title=The Interpretation of Illness|publisher=Purdue University Press| isbn=9780911198881| page=27| passage=For Groddeck, the '''it''' is given, unknowable, and he does not try to conceptualize drives or forces. Early life and sexuality permeate {{...}}}}
# {{alt case|en|It||the [[id]]}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Charis Charalampous|title=Rethinking the Mind-Body Relationship in Early Modern Literature, Philosophy, and Medicine: The Renaissance of the Body| publisher=Routledge| isbn=9781317584209| page=36| passage={{...}} thus reversing the roles of the I and the '''it''', the former now occupying the place of the latter and vice versa. An awareness of our bisubjective nature (it and me) requires thus an I as a third term that slides between&nbsp;{{...}}}}

